<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/undated">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crusades</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> were a series of religious wars launched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latins">Latin Christians</a> from the late eleventh century, chiefly to capture and hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> took advantage of the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a>. The crusades shaped relations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the Islamic world for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crusades.jpg" alt="Crusades" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Combat_deuxi%C3%A8me_croisade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
